<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        This file exists for one reason, and it is the same reason the GTK build has a stylesheet: a
        mixer strip has to be short enough that all sixteen parts of a General MIDI score are on
        screen together. The mixer answers "which part is that" while the music is playing, and a
        strip you have to scroll to find has already lost the moment; muting the wrong one and
        scrolling back costs more than the answer was worth.

        Stock WinUI metrics are worse than stock GTK's were. A ListViewItem has a 40px minimum, and a
        Slider is 32px tall before anything is put in it - taller on its own than the whole strip is
        meant to be. Sixteen stock rows want some 900px of list, which on a 1080p screen is more than
        the window gets once the title bar and the transport are taken out. The rules below bring a
        strip to about 32px, so sixteen fit in roughly 520px.

        Everything here is scoped to the mixer by being applied through explicit styles rather than
        by overriding the theme resources globally: a 4px slider is right on a strip and wrong on the
        transport's gain, and the export bar in the transport needed the opposite correction.
    -->

    <!--
        The row container is a floor, not a suggestion. ListViewItem carries a minimum height and its
        own padding, and neither reacts to the strip inside getting shorter. Zeroing both hands the
        vertical spacing to the strip's own margins, which is the one place it can be reasoned about.

        HorizontalContentAlignment is Stretch because the default leaves the strip at its natural
        width and every width threshold in PartRow would then measure the content rather than the
        row - the faders would never be dropped no matter how narrow the window got.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="MixerRowStyle" TargetType="ListViewItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,0" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Mute and solo are one letter each, so a stock button spends most of its height on padding
        around a glyph half that tall. At 22px they are still comfortably clickable, and they are
        what decides the height of the whole strip since nothing else in it is taller than a line of
        text.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="MixerToggleStyle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="22" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="22" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="26" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The faders, which stock are by a wide margin the tallest thing on a strip: 14px of margin
        above and below a 4px track, 32px in total.

        The margins are what carry the height, not the track, so they are what has to come down - and
        the thumb has to come with them or it clips. At 6/4/6 the control measures 16px, comfortably
        under the 22px the toggles above already set as the strip's floor, so adding faders costs no
        height at all.

        SliderHorizontalHeight has to be set as well as the margins. It is the control's own height
        and does not follow from them; left at 32 it would hold the strip open on its own while the
        track sat in the middle of the empty space.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="MixerFaderStyle" TargetType="Slider">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="60" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="IsThumbToolTipEnabled" Value="False" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The metrics themselves are not here, because they cannot be: Resources is not a
        DependencyProperty and a Setter cannot target it. They live in PartRow's own resource scope
        instead, which is the right place regardless - the faders resolve them up the tree, and
        scoping them to the row is what keeps a 4px track from also being applied to the transport's
        gain slider, where it would be wrong.
    -->

</ResourceDictionary>
